  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\,{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}.\n$$\n\nFind the series expansion of ${f}^{\u2032}(x).$\n\n${f}^{\u2032}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{n}$",
  "worked_solution": "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+\u22ef+{{\\frac{2\\,{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}}_{\u23df}}_{\\text{the\u00a0}n\\text{th term}}+\u22ef.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=2x+2{x}^{2}+\u22ef+{{\\frac{2n(n+1){x}^{n}}{(n+1)!}}_{\u23df}}_{\\text{the\u00a0}n\\text{th term}}+\u22ef.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(n+1){x}^{n}}{(n+1)!} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n!}{x}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
